{
  "wireMagic": "pb3",
  "pens": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wsWinStyles": [ {
  
  } ],
  "wpWinPositions": [ {
  
  } ],
  "events": [ {
    "tStartMs": 359,
    "dDurationMs": 4081,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We often think of quantum mechanics as only affecting only the smallest scales of reality,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 4440,
    "dDurationMs": 3850,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with classical reality taking over at some\nintermediate level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 8290,
    "dDurationMs": 5759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in his 1944 book, What is Life?, the quantum physicist Erwin Schrödinger suggested that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 14049,
    "dDurationMs": 5641,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“incredibly small groups of atoms, much\ntoo small to display exact statistical laws,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 19690,
    "dDurationMs": 6050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "do play a dominating role in the very orderly\nand lawful events within a living organism.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 25740,
    "dDurationMs": 4200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Schrodinger was a visionary - and perhaps\nvery specifically in this case."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 29940,
    "dDurationMs": 4210,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Because it turns out we might need all the\nweirdness of quantum mechanics to explain"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 34150,
    "dDurationMs": 1184,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "birds."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 40640,
    "dDurationMs": 3280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In the summer of 1943, a spy from the UK parachuted"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 43920,
    "dDurationMs": 2920,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "into Vienne, a French town 20 kilometers south of Lyon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 46840,
    "dDurationMs": 5980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There, the spy learned critical information\nfrom the French Resistance about la Milice,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 52820,
    "dDurationMs": 5090,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a Vichy paramilitary group with orders to\nround up Jewish people for concentration camps"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 57910,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and to assassinate Resistance leaders."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 59990,
    "dDurationMs": 5580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "To deliver the information back to London\nsecurely and rapidly, the spy was sent with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 65570,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an unusual piece of technology: a pigeon named All Alone."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 71680,
    "dDurationMs": 4310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The spy attached his message to All Alone and set her free."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 75990,
    "dDurationMs": 7020,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "With unerring accuracy and speed, All Alone\ncovered the 481 miles back to her perch in"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 83010,
    "dDurationMs": 2520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Staines, England in less than 24 hours."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 85530,
    "dDurationMs": 6980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For her service, she was awarded the Dickin\nMedal—the Victoria Cross for animals."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 92510,
    "dDurationMs": 4180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "While All Alone’s flight was undoubtedly\nheroic, she was not unique in her navigational"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 96690,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "abilities."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 97690,
    "dDurationMs": 5520,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "By some estimates, carrier pigeons were 98% reliable in delivering messages during the war."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 103210,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And pigeons are far from alone, and plenty of other species of migratory birds also have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 108350,
    "dDurationMs": 4629,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the uncanny ability to make their way home across hundreds, or even thousands of miles."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 112979,
    "dDurationMs": 5721,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do they do it and what does quantum mechanics have to do with all of this?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 118700,
    "dDurationMs": 5430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Over short distances and on familiar paths, birds can use familiar benchmarks for guidance."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 124130,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Across long distances, birds often rely on\nthe sun or even the stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 128550,
    "dDurationMs": 6769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But All Alone and other birds manage just\nfine at night and when it’s overcast, seemingly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 135319,
    "dDurationMs": 4481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pulled to their destinations like iron filings\nto a magnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 139800,
    "dDurationMs": 2630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And that may not be so far from the truth."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 142430,
    "dDurationMs": 5380,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The first proposal that birds have a “magnetoreception” that they navigate by the Earth’s magnetic"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 147810,
    "dDurationMs": 5009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field came from the Russian zoologist Alexander von Middendorf back in 1855."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 152819,
    "dDurationMs": 6321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A hundred years later, definitive evidence\ncame in the 1960s when German biologists Wolfgang"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 159140,
    "dDurationMs": 5870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Wiltschko and Friedrich Merkel applied magnetic fields to enclosures with European robins,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 165010,
    "dDurationMs": 3170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "preventing them from navigating properly."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 168180,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How exactly birds detect magnetic fields remains an open question."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 172870,
    "dDurationMs": 4550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Some theories have suggested that birds have iron structures in their beaks which help"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 177420,
    "dDurationMs": 6375,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "them orient; others have proposed electrically charged fluids sloshing around in the inner ear."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 183795,
    "dDurationMs": 5905,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But today we’re going to talk about an increasingly favored hypothesis, and surely the most interesting"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 189700,
    "dDurationMs": 6259,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "one: the idea that proposes birds can in a sense see the Earth’s magnetic field due"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 195959,
    "dDurationMs": 3971,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to quantum weirdness happening inside their eyes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 199930,
    "dDurationMs": 3911,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Before we get into all the cool quantum stuff, a quick review on Earth’s magnetic field"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 203841,
    "dDurationMs": 1178,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "is in order."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 205019,
    "dDurationMs": 3771,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For a more thorough explanation, we have an episode on how that field sometimes flips"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 208790,
    "dDurationMs": 3180,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "direction - which I guess drives birds crazy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 211970,
    "dDurationMs": 5440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This “geomagnetic” field is generated\nby the convective motion in Earth’s outer"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 217410,
    "dDurationMs": 4480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "core - which is a churning liquid mass of\nwhite-hot nickel and iron."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 221890,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The result is a dipole field, similar to that\nof a bar magnet: two poles connected by force"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 226730,
    "dDurationMs": 2539,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "lines forming a sort of cage around the planet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 229269,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At the poles, the force lines are roughly\nvertical compared to the surface; at the equator,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 233950,
    "dDurationMs": 1440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "they’re parallel."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 235390,
    "dDurationMs": 3500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At any point on the surface of the earth,\nour geomagnetic field can be described with"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 238890,
    "dDurationMs": 1379,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "just a few properties."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 240269,
    "dDurationMs": 5071,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s their vertical orientation or “inclination”, so whether they’re parallel to the ground"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 245340,
    "dDurationMs": 1740,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "or point into the ground."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 247080,
    "dDurationMs": 5189,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And their horizontal orientation, or angle\nrelative to lines of longitude."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 252269,
    "dDurationMs": 3940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This “declination” points towards the\nmagnetic poles, which are offset from the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 256209,
    "dDurationMs": 2840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "true poles defined by Earth’s rotational\naxis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 259049,
    "dDurationMs": 6110,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There’s also a directional arrow attached\nto the lines - as in one direction pointing “north”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 265159,
    "dDurationMs": 1980,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and the other “south”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 267139,
    "dDurationMs": 3800,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And finally there’s an intensity of the\nfield, represented by how close together the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 270939,
    "dDurationMs": 1471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "field lines are."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 272410,
    "dDurationMs": 6599,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "At Earth’s surface it’s about 30 microTesla,\nwhich is about 100 times weaker than a fridge"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 279009,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 280009,
    "dDurationMs": 6430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As far as we know, birds can sense the orientation of the field lines, but NOT their polarity"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 286439,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "arrows."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 287439,
    "dDurationMs": 4671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So they can tell the direction to the nearest\npole, but don’t know WHICH pole it is."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 292110,
    "dDurationMs": 4250,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In principle it’s easy to come up with ways\nto sense a magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 296360,
    "dDurationMs": 4389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Magnetic fields exert a force on a moving\nor rotating charged particle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 300749,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "An electron, for example, can be thought of\nas a spinning charge, and magnetic fields"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 305409,
    "dDurationMs": 3000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "can cause that spin to flip direction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 308409,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Typical bar magnets like the one found in\na compass needle aure ferromagnets, and their"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 313079,
    "dDurationMs": 4471,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "magnetic fields come from countless electrons\nwith aligned spins."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 317550,
    "dDurationMs": 4970,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "External magnetic fields tug on those electrons\nresulting in a force that can swivel the compass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 322520,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "needle."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 323660,
    "dDurationMs": 5280,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But you need a lot of electrons to register\nEarth’s extremely weak field - far more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 328940,
    "dDurationMs": 4789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "than you could fit into the microscopic structures within a bird’s eye."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 333729,
    "dDurationMs": 4610,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And this is where quantum mechanics comes in."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 338339,
    "dDurationMs": 7130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The insight came back in 1978, when a German biophysicist named Klaus Schulten was studying"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 345469,
    "dDurationMs": 2220,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“radical pairs.”"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 347689,
    "dDurationMs": 6551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A radical is any atom or molecule with a lone electron in an outermost or valence shell."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 354240,
    "dDurationMs": 5769,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A radical pair is, well, a pair of radicals\n- connected in a very special way."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 360009,
    "dDurationMs": 3141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Their unpaired valence electrons are entangled."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 363150,
    "dDurationMs": 4479,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "A quick review of quantum entanglement is\nin order here, although we’re talked about"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 367629,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "it before.."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 368629,
    "dDurationMs": 4870,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When two particles are entangled, it means\none or more of their quantum properties are"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 373499,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "correlated."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 374499,
    "dDurationMs": 4440,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This can lead to all sorts of weird effects,\nincluding an apparent faster-than-light influence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 378939,
    "dDurationMs": 4760,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- measure the property of one particle and\nyou instantaneously influence the entangled"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 383699,
    "dDurationMs": 1120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "partner."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 384819,
    "dDurationMs": 4551,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Today we’re interested in a different aspect\nof the weirdness of entanglement."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 389370,
    "dDurationMs": 5529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The entangled properties are the quantum spins of the two valence electrons in two separate radical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 394899,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "molecules."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 395899,
    "dDurationMs": 4230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are four possible combinations for the spins: the first is"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 400129,
    "dDurationMs": 4331,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "the so-called singlet state, where the spins\nare pointing in opposite directions - we'll"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 404460,
    "dDurationMs": 2370,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "call them up and down."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 406830,
    "dDurationMs": 7008,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We don't know which electron is up or down, and this is undefinedness is part of the whole entanglement deal."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 413838,
    "dDurationMs": 4652,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Simply “opposite each other” becomes this single state - hence “singlet”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 418490,
    "dDurationMs": 3709,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The other three states are when the electrons have the same spin direction - either both"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 422199,
    "dDurationMs": 4330,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "up, both down, or a quantum superposition\nof both at the same time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 426529,
    "dDurationMs": 4461,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "These three are energetically equivalent, so together they make up a triplet state."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 430990,
    "dDurationMs": 4049,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you have just one radical, its valence\nelectron spin tends to stay fixed until"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 435039,
    "dDurationMs": 6875,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "disturbed by its environment. And Earth's magnetic field isn't strong enough to influence spin in that time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 441914,
    "dDurationMs": 5585,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But in a radical pair spin states will oscillate between the singlet and triplet states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 447499,
    "dDurationMs": 5540,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "They do that evenly in the absence of a magnetic field - 75% of the time in the triplet state"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 453039,
    "dDurationMs": 2060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "and 25% in the singlet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 455099,
    "dDurationMs": 4470,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But even a weak magnetic field like the Earth's can affect the amount of time the radical"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 459569,
    "dDurationMs": 1530,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "pair spends in these states."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 461099,
    "dDurationMs": 4671,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If that field has the correct orientation,\nthe system will spend more time in the triplet"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 465770,
    "dDurationMs": 2989,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "state - with both electron spins aligned in\nthe"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 468759,
    "dDurationMs": 4261,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "same direction, and less in the singlet state\nwhere they have opposite alignments."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 473020,
    "dDurationMs": 6399,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "OK, so we have a mechanism to influence two tiny electrons - but a few questions remain:"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 479419,
    "dDurationMs": 4411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "how is the radical pair produced, how long\ndoes the entanglement need to last in order"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 483830,
    "dDurationMs": 4369,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to be influenced by Earth's magnetic field,\nand how does the simple slipping of electron"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 488199,
    "dDurationMs": 3391,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "spins go on to give the bird magnetovision?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 491590,
    "dDurationMs": 4060,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Klaus Schulten and colleagues proposed the mechanism that has remained mostly unchanged"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 495650,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to this day."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 496650,
    "dDurationMs": 3799,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It goes like this: birds have some protein\nin their eyes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 500449,
    "dDurationMs": 4131,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "When light hits the protein, it knocks an\nelectron off an attached molecule that goes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 504580,
    "dDurationMs": 1670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "onto an adjacent molecule."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 506250,
    "dDurationMs": 4789,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The two molecules now share a pair of entangled electrons—they become a radical pair for"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 511039,
    "dDurationMs": 1430,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "a short period of time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 512469,
    "dDurationMs": 3611,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then they quickly react to produce some chemical byproducts."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 516080,
    "dDurationMs": 5009,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But there’s the key - those byproducts are\nsensitive to the spin state of the valence"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 521089,
    "dDurationMs": 2601,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "electrons at the time of the reaction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 523690,
    "dDurationMs": 5509,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So during the short lifespan of the radical\npair, its valence spin state can be modified"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 529199,
    "dDurationMs": 5140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "if the bird changes the orientation of its\nhead relative to the Earth’s magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 534339,
    "dDurationMs": 5281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That leads to a changing yield of different\npossible byproducts across the bird’s eyes."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 539620,
    "dDurationMs": 5630,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That could lead to a true visual sense of\nmagnetic field orientation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 545250,
    "dDurationMs": 5410,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We won’t delve too far into the biology\nor chemistry here, but there is a protein"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 550660,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that does all of this - it’s called cryptochrome."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 555160,
    "dDurationMs": 4489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Experiments since Schulten’s proposal have shown that it’s possible to affect cryptochromes"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 559649,
    "dDurationMs": 6401,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "with a weak magnetic field and get that characteristic change of rate of chemical reaction."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 566050,
    "dDurationMs": 5959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Notably, fruit flies without the gene for\ncryptochromes are unable to navigate."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 572009,
    "dDurationMs": 4700,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Although birds do have cryptochromes, the\nmechanism itself has not been directly observed"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 576709,
    "dDurationMs": 3851,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "in a bird, and it remains only the most likely\nexplanation."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 580560,
    "dDurationMs": 5050,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This “avian compass” presents a tantalizing\npossibility of quantum biology."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 585610,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "It’s strange to think of quantum effects\nbeing relevant in living organisms."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 589190,
    "dDurationMs": 4040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Normally, to observe the strange behavior\nof the quantum world we need to perform incredibly"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 593230,
    "dDurationMs": 6140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "careful experiments in highly controlled environments - ideally isolated systems of very few particles,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 599370,
    "dDurationMs": 2959,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "perhaps in a vacuum or near absolute zero\ntemperature."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 602329,
    "dDurationMs": 5241,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Not in the warm, wet, and messily macroscopic environment of a living organism."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 607570,
    "dDurationMs": 3491,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum entanglement is very quickly destroyed in such environments - but birds may have"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 611061,
    "dDurationMs": 1338,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "found a workaround."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 612399,
    "dDurationMs": 4141,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The radical pairs only need to stay entangled for a microsecond for this mechanism to do"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 616540,
    "dDurationMs": 4219,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "its job - because after the entanglement is\ndestroyed the subsequent chemical reactions"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 620759,
    "dDurationMs": 3830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "remember the quantum state, and so remember the magnetic field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 624589,
    "dDurationMs": 2810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So is this true quantum biology?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 627399,
    "dDurationMs": 4670,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Earlier this year, Peter Hore, a physical\nchemist at Oxford co-authored a paper that"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 632069,
    "dDurationMs": 1251,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "seems to answer that."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 633320,
    "dDurationMs": 4559,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The team’s calculations showed that only\na full quantum description of the process"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 637879,
    "dDurationMs": 3061,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "could produce the required sensitivity to\nmagnetic fields."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 640940,
    "dDurationMs": 4660,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If, for example, the valence electrons were\njust interacting due to their magnetic fields"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 645600,
    "dDurationMs": 5590,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- so-called spin-spin interactions - rather\nthan true entangled states - their spin state"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 651190,
    "dDurationMs": 2860,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "wouldn’t be sensitive enough to detect Earth’s field."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 654050,
    "dDurationMs": 4450,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So Erwin Schrodinger’s ideas about quantum mechanics influencing living organisms may"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 658500,
    "dDurationMs": 1269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "be right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 659769,
    "dDurationMs": 5620,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And quantum magnetoreception in birds isn’t the only example of what we sometimes call"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 665389,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "“quantum biology”."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 666389,
    "dDurationMs": 5221,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "We know for sure that it happens in some cases - like the quantum tunneling that drives enzyme"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 671610,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "catalysis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 672610,
    "dDurationMs": 4120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are other contentious, but intriguing\ncases - like the idea that long-range quantum"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 676730,
    "dDurationMs": 2349,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "coherence may drive photosynthesis."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 679079,
    "dDurationMs": 5521,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And there are some highly contentious ideas - like quantum entanglement in the brain’s"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 684600,
    "dDurationMs": 3029,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "microtubule proteins as a key ingredient in\nhuman consciousness."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 687629,
    "dDurationMs": 4591,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The quantum magnetoreception of the avian compass sits somewhere in the middle - not"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 692220,
    "dDurationMs": 2429,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "yet proved, but more and more favoured."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 694649,
    "dDurationMs": 4321,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Klaus Schulten’s microtubules must have\nbeen working overtime to hit on such a brilliant"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 698970,
    "dDurationMs": 1140,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "insight."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 700110,
    "dDurationMs": 5320,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "He may still be proved wrong, but it’s a\nbeautiful idea: pigeons and geese and albatrosses,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 705430,
    "dDurationMs": 4699,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "swallows - european especially - birds of\nmany a feather using quantum physics to flock"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 710129,
    "dDurationMs": 6051,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "together to navigate the hidden lines of a geomagnetic space time."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 716180,
    "dDurationMs": 3010,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "As always, a huge shoutout to our supporters on Patreon."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 719190,
    "dDurationMs": 1810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Thanks for having our back, guys."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 721000,
    "dDurationMs": 4829,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And today I want to give an extra extra special thank you to Hank S, who supports us at the"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 725829,
    "dDurationMs": 1481,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "quasar level."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 727310,
    "dDurationMs": 5759,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Hank - you once asked me a question about\nspace contraction in special relativity and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 733069,
    "dDurationMs": 4411,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "whether or not a spaceship could travel so\nfast it bumped into its own rear end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 737480,
    "dDurationMs": 5529,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I couldn't give you a satisfactory answer\nat the time - and so to show our appreciation"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 743009,
    "dDurationMs": 2130,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I'm not going to give you one now."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 745139,
    "dDurationMs": 4281,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Instead I'm going to do a whole episode on\nsome of the weirder paradoxes in relativity,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 749420,
    "dDurationMs": 3310,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that I think will make it all totally clear."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 752730,
    "dDurationMs": 3580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Or confuse matters far worse- it remains to\nbe seen."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 756310,
    "dDurationMs": 4160,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, your support means everything to us, so thank you."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 760470,
    "dDurationMs": 5300,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I just wanted let you know about PBS's new\nscience show Weathered."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 765770,
    "dDurationMs": 3790,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "If you feel like the weather is getting weirder\nlately, well, you’re right."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 769560,
    "dDurationMs": 4269,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And if you want to know the science of why\nthat's the case, join meteorologist and host"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 773829,
    "dDurationMs": 5200,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Maiya May on Weathered to explore why hurricanes, wildfires and other extreme weather is more"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 779029,
    "dDurationMs": 3841,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "frequent and intense than ever, as well as what you can do to prepare."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 782870,
    "dDurationMs": 4389,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Weathered is on PBS Terra and you can check out the link in our description."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 787259,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Okay."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 788259,
    "dDurationMs": 4341,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Last week we talked about a possible new type of supernova - the black dwarf or iron star"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 792600,
    "dDurationMs": 4940,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "supernova, which may be the very last explosions at the end of our universe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 797540,
    "dDurationMs": 4830,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "I was gratified that you were all so excited\nabout this thing that no one will ever, ever"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 802370,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "observe."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 803370,
    "dDurationMs": 5600,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Kroy H correctly summized there there are\nstill more types of supernova to talk about."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 808970,
    "dDurationMs": 2000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Indeed there are - many in fact."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 810970,
    "dDurationMs": 5070,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "You have types 1 and 2, each with multiple\nsub-types, then the rarer types 3 through"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 816040,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "5."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 817040,
    "dDurationMs": 4690,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "In typical astronomy style, the classifications are entirely based on the observational properties"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 821730,
    "dDurationMs": 4549,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "- what elements are observed as emission or absorption lines, the way the light decays"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 826279,
    "dDurationMs": 1691,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "over time, and other things."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 827970,
    "dDurationMs": 4190,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "But really there are two main scenarios - either the core of a very massive star collapses"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 832160,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "after expending its fuel - that’s a type\n2 supernova - or the remnant of a lower mass"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 837000,
    "dDurationMs": 5040,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "star - a white dwarf - gains extra mass and\nexplodes - that’s a type 1a."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 842040,
    "dDurationMs": 3489,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For more detail we would indeed need a whole episode"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 845529,
    "dDurationMs": 2240,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Quantum fields asks what about neutron stars."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 847769,
    "dDurationMs": 1170,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "What are their final fates?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 848939,
    "dDurationMs": 3781,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "This all depends on some physics that we haven’t nailed down yet."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 852720,
    "dDurationMs": 5059,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "There are two broad scenarios - first, if\nprotons do NOT decay then quantum tunneling"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 857779,
    "dDurationMs": 5500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will cause the neutron star to collapse into\na black hole over an absurdly long timescale"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 863279,
    "dDurationMs": 5091,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "of 10^10^20-70 or so years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 868370,
    "dDurationMs": 5810,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "And then those black holes evaporate into\nradiation on a comparitifly short timescale."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 874180,
    "dDurationMs": 5420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "On the other hand if protons DO decay then\nthe small proton content of neutron stars"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 879600,
    "dDurationMs": 4500,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "will decay into pions and neutrinos, leaking\naway some of the mass of the neutron star."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 884100,
    "dDurationMs": 4840,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The neutron star will then expand, allowing\nsome neutrons to convert back into protons,"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 888940,
    "dDurationMs": 1680,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "which can themselves decay."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 890620,
    "dDurationMs": 4560,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "The Star may go through a phase as a white\n- or perhaps now black dwarf and will continue"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 895180,
    "dDurationMs": 2120,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "to evaporate due to proton decay."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 897300,
    "dDurationMs": 5979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "That last option is described in the book\nfive ages of the universe by Fred Adams and"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 903279,
    "dDurationMs": 1290,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Gregory Laughlin"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 904569,
    "dDurationMs": 5171,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Broken silence asks whether all this means\nthat Bertrand Russell’s teapot will also"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 909740,
    "dDurationMs": 1099,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "come to an end."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 910839,
    "dDurationMs": 5011,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "For those who aren’t familiar Russell’s\nteapot is a hypothetical piece of chinaware"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 915850,
    "dDurationMs": 4580,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "that orbits the sun between the earth and\nmars, and which science has yet to prove doesn’t"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 920430,
    "dDurationMs": 1000,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "exist."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 921430,
    "dDurationMs": 3990,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "So yeah, Russell’s teapot either evaporates\nas its protons decay, or quantum tunnels into"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 925420,
    "dDurationMs": 3950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "an iron teapot - is that a kettle?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 929370,
    "dDurationMs": 5230,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Many of you pointed out how hard it is to\nwrap your heads around the sort of timescales"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 934600,
    "dDurationMs": 2950,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "we talked about in that episode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 937550,
    "dDurationMs": 4420,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Like 10^32000 years for the smallest iron\ndwarf to explode."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 941970,
    "dDurationMs": 2080,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "C’mon guys, it’s easy."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 944050,
    "dDurationMs": 5979,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Step one - first imagine the passage of 10^31999 years."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 950029,
    "dDurationMs": 2480,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Then imagine that happening 10 times."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 952509,
    "dDurationMs": 4681,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "How do you imagine 10^319999 years you ask?"
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 957190,
    "dDurationMs": 7550,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Easy, start by imagining 10^31998 years and then do THAT 10 times."
    } ]
  }, {
    "tStartMs": 964740,
    "dDurationMs": 939,
    "segs": [ {
      "utf8": "Anyway, you get the picture."
    } ]
  } ]
}
